<dl><dt>Can an F&amp;O loss be set off against salary?</dt><dd>No — at either stage. In the loss year, Section 71(2A) expressly bars a business loss from being set off against income under the head Salaries. In later years, Section 72 confines the carried-forward loss to business income, which excludes salary again.</dd><dt>Is there a current-year exception?</dt><dd>No. Section 71(2A) opens with "Notwithstanding" and overrides the general inter-head rule in Section 71(1). Salary is the one head a business loss can never reach.</dd><dt>What can an F&amp;O loss be set off against?</dt><dd>House property income, capital gains (both short-term and long-term) and other sources such as FD interest, in the same year under Section 71. Also against any other business income under Section 70.</dd><dt>What about a carried-forward F&amp;O loss?</dt><dd>Under Section 72 it can be set off only against business income, for up to 8 assessment years.</dd><dt>Do I still have to report the loss?</dt><dd>Yes. Reporting it in ITR-3 by the due date — 31 August 2026 for a non-audit filer — is what preserves your right to carry it forward.</dd></dl>
