[{"question": "Can F&O loss be set off against salary income?", "answer": "No. Section 71(2A) of the Income Tax Act 1961 states that where the result under \"Profits and gains of business or profession\" is a loss and the assessee has income under \"Salaries\", that loss shall not be set off against salary. This applies in the loss year itself, not only to brought-forward losses. The loss can, however, be set off against house property income, capital gains and other sources under Section 71."}, {"question": "Which loss can be adjusted against salary?", "answer": "Very few. House property loss can be set off against salary under the old regime, capped at Rs 2,00,000 a year under Section 71(3A) — and the default new regime under Section 115BAC removes even that. Business loss, including F&O loss, and capital loss cannot be adjusted against salary at all."}, {"question": "Can F&O loss be set off against capital gains or STCG?", "answer": "Yes. F&O loss is a business loss, and Section 71 permits a current-year business loss to be set off against both short-term and long-term capital gains. For a salaried trader this is usually the largest relief available. The reverse is not true: a capital loss cannot be set off against business income."}, {"question": "Is it mandatory to show F&O loss in ITR for salaried people?", "answer": "Yes. F&O activity is business income, so the return must disclose it regardless of profit or loss. Non-disclosure risks a mismatch notice, because your trades are reported to the department through AIS and broker statements — and an unreported loss cannot be carried forward."}, {"question": "Where to show F&O loss in ITR for a salaried person?", "answer": "In ITR-3. The computation goes in Schedule BP, the current-year inter-head set-off in Schedule CYLA, and the unabsorbed balance in Schedule CFL. Salary continues to be reported in Schedule S and is not reduced by the F&O loss."}, {"question": "What if I don't report F&O loss?", "answer": "You lose the right to carry it forward, and you risk a notice. Since the loss cannot reduce salary anyway, taxpayers often assume reporting is pointless — but the carry forward is worth real money if you trade again profitably within 8 years."}, {"question": "Can a salaried person do F&O trading?", "answer": "Yes, subject to your employer's own conduct rules. Tax law does not restrict it. It does change your filing obligation, because F&O makes you a person with business income and moves you from ITR-1 to ITR-3. It also means opting for the old regime now requires Form 10-IEA by the due date."}, {"question": "Can we set off F&O loss against business income?", "answer": "Yes, and this is the widest route available. Under Section 70, F&O loss is set off against any other business income first, including speculative profits such as intraday equity gains. Any unabsorbed balance carried forward under Section 72 can also only meet business income."}, {"question": "F&O loss salary se adjust hota hai kya?", "answer": "Nahi. Section 71(2A) ke under business loss — F&O loss included — salary income se adjust nahi ho sakta, na current year mein aur na carry forward ke baad. F&O loss sirf business income, capital gains, house property income aur other sources se set off hota hai. Bacha hua loss ITR-3 mein due date se pehle (AY 2026-27 ke liye 31 August 2026) file karke 8 saal tak carry forward karein."}]
